      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 2
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Method"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "orderedList",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Blanch the pork belly in boiling water for 3 minutes. Drain and pat dry. This removes impurities and gives the pork a cleaner flavour."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Heat the sugar in a Dutch oven over medium heat without stirring. Swirl gently when it starts to melt. Cook until it is a deep amber and smells slightly bitter."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Carefully add the pork belly to the caramel. Toss to coat. The caramel will seize up . this is normal. Stir over medium heat until it melts again."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Add the garlic, shallots, and ginger. Stir for 1 minute."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Pour in the coconut water and fish sauce. Add just enough water to almost cover the pork. Bring to the boil."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Reduce the heat to low. Add the peeled hard-boiled eggs. Simmer uncovered for 60 to 75 minutes until the pork is very tender and the sauce has reduced and thickened."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Taste the sauce and adjust with more fish sauce if needed. Garnish with spring onion and serve with steamed rice."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "troubleshooter",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "troubleshooterItem",
            "attrs": {
              "symptom": "The pork is still tough after an hour.",
              "cause": "The heat was too high and the braise was boiling rather than simmering.",
              "fix": "Keep the heat at the lowest possible simmer. The pork needs slow, gentle heat to break down the collagen."
            }
          },
          {
            "type": "troubleshooterItem",
            "attrs": {
              "symptom": "The eggs are rubbery and dry.",
              "cause": "The eggs were over-boiled before being added to the braise.",
              "fix": "Boil eggs for exactly 10 minutes for a firm but not chalky yolk. Add them to the braise for the last 30 minutes only."
            }
          },
          {
            "type": "troubleshooterItem",
            "attrs": {
              "symptom": "The sauce is too thin at the end.",
              "cause": "Too much liquid was added or the lid was left on during cooking.",
              "fix": "Cook uncovered to allow evaporation. If still thin, increase heat for the last 10 minutes to reduce the sauce."
            }
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 2
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Where this dish lives"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Thịt kho is one of the most universally loved dishes in Vietnamese home cooking. "
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "It is eaten across the country but is especially associated with Tết, the Lunar New Year. "
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Families make a large clay pot and eat it over the first few days of the new year. "
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Southern versions use coconut water in the braise, which is different from the northern style. "
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "The dish has a place on almost every Vietnamese family table during celebrations."
          }
        ]
      }
    ]
  }
}
